// [ssTEA Use Of Function Pointers.note]search in ssTEA source files for a reference to this file.
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Tbis note tries to explain the complex software concept at the heart of mesa Software: pointers.

   In a software programming language, the compiler knows a lot of information about any variable.

     Each variable declared has:    a name, a data type or size, a lifetime, an address

   When the software refers to the name, the compiler knows the other information based on the name.
   A variable that can contain the address, and be used in accessing memory, is called a pointer.
   The pointer variable has a name, a value, a lifetime and an address.  The value is an address.

   Devices within the view of the processor are accessed using pointers assigned published values.
   Data allocation is typically managed by the Host and so every reference is through a pointer.
   Host data allocation and transfer requires pointers to data with appropriate permission, access.

   The definition of the memory pointed at is distinct from the value in the pointer over Time:
     software can't return a pointer to a variable on the stack because the allocation goes away.
     all memory, and any device which appear to be memory, has attributes distinct from the value.
     a variable with an incorrect or illegal value has no effect until the value is used to access.

   The end result of separating the address from the attributes creates a danger for software: the
   value in a pointer can be anything, the reference is independent of any named variable known.
   The code can be generated and running in the App on the mesa, referencing memory using the value
   of the pointer given, then: an illegal value is placed into the variable, then the ATTEMPT to use
   the pointer is detected BEFORE the code executes, a hard fault occurs, the processor is halted.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   .\SimpleSal\ssTEA\ssTEA_data_dcl.h                                    "pointer to void" data type
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The Host Language promises that a "pointer to void" is as large as any other pointer, so using
   a variable of type "pointer to void" will ensure that a "pointer to X" will fit in the variable.
   This usually signals shenanigans are afoot: why hide the pointer's type from the Host Language?
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   .\SimpleSal\ssTEA\ssTEA_data_dcl.h                              "pointer to a function" data type
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Functions.  A function's address may be used just like any location value for any type, the type
   of the variable holding the address of a function is "function pointer", similar to "int pointer".
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   ssTEA does not require a user to understand function pointers; only assigning a value, an address
   of a User function, to a variable in an ssTEA data structure is required when Events are defined.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   At the end of this note is a long description of how a compiler knows about things and learns
   about the dependency between things in the process of creating the software the processor runs.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   This declaration is required for a declaration of the Event function and the types of parameters.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   typedef pVoid_t                 ssE_pVoidAgencyParams_t;
   #define pVoidAgencyParamsNull   ((ssE_pVoidAgencyParams_t) NULL)
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The key to granting agency to an event is the use of the function pointer to cause the processor
   to "call" this software.  In its purest sense, this is Granting Agency to an FSM Signal handler.

   Management of events occurs at the API level, and when the Event function is actually executed.
   Using words: "EvAg_pEvFunc_t is a pointer to a function with 2 parameters, returning a result.".
   The 1st parameter is what ssE knows about an event; includes a pointer to Agency and App data.
   the 2nd parameter is a pointer to a COPY of the current Time (you cannot change Time arbitrarily).
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   "EvAg_pEvFunc_t" is described as "pointer to function returning result".  An Event is a function,
   with this specific definition. A function has an address and can be referred to with a pointer.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The bridge between "an event" and "a ssHL-language function" is here: users of ssTEA are given descriptions
   of Events, user functions are called Events; technically, the Event pointer is to a ssHL-language function.
   An Event is pointed to by the function's address, and the function pointer points to an Event.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   typedef ssE_EvResult_t(* EvAg_pEvFunc_t) (ssE_pVoidAgencyParams_t, ssT_pTime_t);

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   as with other pointer types, the value of a variable is NULL when it does not point to an object.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   #define pEvFuncNull         ((EvAg_pEvFunc_t) NULL)
//
//
//
//
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   A key ssHL-language compiler issue analysis and explanation, if you can consider you are the compiler:

   The order of declaration of types and variables is as required by ssHL-language, as revealed by the coder.
   You are in the Event declaration file, which the Agency declarations will need to define Agency.
   You have not read the Agency file, so you cannot refer to the type of the value given to the
   event at the Time it occurs, unless that type is declared at the ssE level.  If the coder solves
   by changing the order of declaration, they will find that the OTHER type has not been declared.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   problem: the type of the parameter to an event function is not defined yet
      compiler writer's solution:
        Emit obscure message about "decl", means coder used an undefined type in a typedef, see www.
      coder's solution to what is essentially a compile-time problem:
        in event function declarations and definitions, use pVoid_t as a parameter placeholder.
        the first event action is to translate the parameter (pVoid_t) to an appropriate pointer.
        this does not emit any code, it changes the compiler's view of the type of the pointer.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   problem: if the information provided to the Event is a copy of ssTEA's copy of the User's
   Event Agency Descriptor, there is no the information available to the event about its own Agency.
      compiler's solution:
        I told you that you can't refer to types you have not defined yet.
      coder's solution within the context of always using the most basic ssHL-language features:
        option 1: pass the Agency Desc as parameter, put pointer to Agency Secrets in the Agency Desc.
        option 2: pass Agency Secrets as parameter, have every Event pick up Agency Desc from that.
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   In the context of ssTEA, option 1: it is best to simplify for the minimal user, support tricks.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   problem: option 1 (Event Desc pointer) needs Event Desc defined at the time pEvFunc is typedefed,
      which is exactly the same problem as with using an Agency-level parameter type.  So pVoid_t.
   coder's solution:
      create a nomenclature that refers to voidX at Event level and Agency will use non-voidX.
      Do the same method for parameters, as well as for the pointer to Agency secrets from ssTEA.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   problem: option 1 ssE's view of outside entities is minimal, but events run by ssE are omniscient.
   coder's solution:
      User event software should be the last code compiled, with full knowledge of every layer.
      The RunApi will assign a value in the ssE_AboutEv Descriptor that links to any Secret context data,
        for example, the value might be a pointer to an ssTEA statistical database about the Event.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// S~S  SimpleSal signal ends  S~S
